/* The meridian-crossing test of crates/spacelab/src/zone.rs (Ф31.C): a point is in a zone when
   the crossings of its meridian north of it, by the zone's geodesic edges, have the parity that
   puts it on the north pole's side, or the parity that does not when the pole is outside.

   The ellipsoid x^2/a^2 + y^2/a^2 + z^2/b^2 = 1, b^2 = a^2 (1 - e2), in geodetic coordinates:
       N = a / sqrt(1 - e2 sin^2 phi),  M = a (1 - e2) / (1 - e2 sin^2 phi)^(3/2),
       p(phi, lam) = (N cos phi cos lam, N cos phi sin lam, N (1 - e2) sin phi),
   the outward normal n and the unit east and north vectors eE, eN. A geodesic leaves azimuth
   alpha clockwise from north: its unit tangent is t = cos alpha eN + sin alpha eE. The claims, each
   a residual that must reduce to 0:
     1-2  dp/dphi = M eN and dp/dlam = N cos phi eE: the metric ds^2 = M^2 dphi^2 + rho^2 dlam^2,
          rho = N cos phi the radius of the parallel;
     3    along a unit-speed curve with tangent t, rho dlam/ds = sin alpha (the east component of t);
     4    Clairaut: c = rho sin alpha is constant along a geodesic of a surface of revolution, so
          dlam/ds = c / rho^2 has the sign of c all along it -- the longitude is monotonic on every
          geodesic that is not a meridian (c = 0). With the shortest geodesic's sweep, the reduced
          longitude difference of its ends (|sweep| <= pi, Karney 2013 sect. 4), an edge crosses a
          meridian at most once. The residual is drho/dphi = -M sin phi, the identity that makes
          d(rho sin alpha)/ds = 0 with the geodesic's d alpha/ds = sin alpha tan phi / N;
     5    the same: d(rho sin alpha)/ds with dphi/ds = cos alpha / M and that d alpha/ds;
     6    the left of the direction of travel, seen from outside, is n x t; its north component is
          sin alpha: an edge heading east (sin alpha > 0) has the interior to its north;
     7    rho = a cos beta, tan beta = (1 - f) tan phi: Clairaut on the auxiliary sphere,
          cos beta sin alpha = sin alpha0, so the geodesic's highest reduced latitude has
          cos beta = |sin alpha0| -- the vertex zone.rs widens an edge's latitude range to;
     8    n x t = sin alpha eN - cos alpha eE, the left of the direction of travel in the tangent
          plane: zone.rs measures how far a point p lies across an edge as |(p - c) . (n x t)|, c
          the edge's point on p's meridian, so a point M dphi north of c lies M |dphi| |sin alpha|
          across, to first order.
   Two shortest geodesics meet at most once unless they overlap, which is a theorem and no
   identity: if they met twice, the two arcs between the meetings would both be shortest, and an
   interior sub-arc of a shortest geodesic is its only shortest path.
   derivations/zone_meridian_crossing.gp is the second engine: crossings at 50 digits by quadrature
   on the auxiliary sphere, the reference rows crates/spacelab/src/zone.rs holds. */

declare([a, e2, phi, lam, alpha], real)$
assume(a > 0, e2 > 0, e2 < 1, cos(phi) > 0)$

W : sqrt(1 - e2 * sin(phi)^2)$
N : a / W$
M : a * (1 - e2) / W^3$
p : [N * cos(phi) * cos(lam), N * cos(phi) * sin(lam), N * (1 - e2) * sin(phi)]$
n : [cos(phi) * cos(lam), cos(phi) * sin(lam), sin(phi)]$
eE : [-sin(lam), cos(lam), 0]$
eN : [-sin(phi) * cos(lam), -sin(phi) * sin(lam), cos(phi)]$
sq(v) := v . v$
cross(u, v) := [u[2] * v[3] - u[3] * v[2], u[3] * v[1] - u[1] * v[3], u[1] * v[2] - u[2] * v[1]]$
z(e) := trigsimp(ratsimp(e))$

print("dp/dphi - M eN, squared:", z(sq(diff(p, phi) - M * eN)))$
rho : N * cos(phi)$
print("dp/dlam - rho eE, squared:", z(sq(diff(p, lam) - rho * eE)))$

/* A unit-speed curve: dp/ds = p_phi phi' + p_lam lam' = t. Its east component is rho lam'. */
t : cos(alpha) * eN + sin(alpha) * eE$
phid : cos(alpha) / M$
lamd : sin(alpha) / rho$
print("dp/ds - t, squared:", z(sq(diff(p, phi) * phid + diff(p, lam) * lamd - t)))$

print("drho/dphi + M sin phi:", z(diff(rho, phi) + M * sin(phi)))$
alphad : sin(alpha) * tan(phi) / N$
print("d(rho sin alpha)/ds along a geodesic:",
      z(diff(rho, phi) * phid * sin(alpha) + rho * cos(alpha) * alphad))$

print("north component of n x t - sin alpha:", z(cross(n, t) . eN - sin(alpha)))$

cb : cos(phi) / sqrt(cos(phi)^2 + (1 - e2) * sin(phi)^2)$
print("rho - a cos beta:", z(rho - a * cb))$

print("n x t - (sin alpha eN - cos alpha eE), squared:",
      z(sq(cross(n, t) - (sin(alpha) * eN - cos(alpha) * eE))))$
